{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>Who appointed you a man.</b> And you are still a lad, yet you wish to be a man. From this we learn that Moses was not yet thirteen years old, for at thirteen years one is called a man. And so they said in the Midrash: Rabbi Chanina said, at twelve years Moses was taken from his father's house. And in these three expressions that they spoke against him, we find that he took a crown: they said to him, 'Who appointed you a man' — we find that he was called a man, as it is said (Numbers 12): ״והאיש משה ענו מאד״; and he was called a prince (שר), as it is said (Numbers 21): ״באר חפרוה שרים כרוה נדיבי העם״; and he was called a judge, as it is said (Exodus 18): ״וישב משה לשפוט את העם״." "<b>Indeed the thing is known.</b> A matter that I had been wondering about has become known to me: why Israel, in exile, more than all the other nations — because among them there is talebearing and evil tongue, and they are not worthy of redemption. And so they said in the Midrash on Psalms: there were young children in the generation of Saul and Samuel who, before they had brought two hairs, would expound the Torah in forty-nine ways as pure and in forty-nine ways as impure, and David would pray for them and say: אתה ה' תשמרם — may You guard their Torah in their hearts; תצרנו מן הדור — this is a generation liable to destruction; and after all this praise they would go out to war and fall — why? Because there were informers (דלטורין) among them. David said: Master of the universe, withdraw Your Shechinah from among them — what is Your Shechinah doing there? It is written: רומה על השמים אלהים. But the generation of Ahab were all idol worshippers, and because there were no informers among them, they would go out to war and prevail, and not a single one of them was killed. This is what Obadiah said to Elijah (I Kings 18): ״הלא הגד לאדני את אשר עשיתי ואחביא מנביאי ה' חמשים חמשים איש במערה ואכלכלם לחם ומים״ — what does לחם ומים teach? It teaches that the water was harder to obtain than the bread. And Elijah stood on Mount Carmel and proclaimed and said, 'I alone am left, a prophet to HaShem,' and all the people knew, yet did not inform King Ahab — because of evil tongue the Shechinah withdraws."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:55:23.317417102Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}